# General Agent Harness project rules

Treat `docs/plans/general-agent-harness.md` as the authoritative implementation
roadmap. Implement one phase at a time and satisfy its objective exit criteria
before starting the next phase.

## Architecture

- Preserve DeepSeek Harness and its Cordis plugin/composition model.
- Prefer changes in this order: `REUSE → EXTEND → EXTRACT → NEW → REPLACE`.
- `REPLACE` requires an ADR proving an additive extension cannot work, plus a
  migration path and regression coverage.
- Keep core provider-agnostic and domain-agnostic. Core must not know about
  Cybersecurity, Coding, Pentesting, Data Science, or a specific model vendor.
- Reuse the existing agent loop, sessions/events, prompt assembly, typed tool
  runtime, model adapters, capability providers, profiles, bundles, presets,
  MCP, jobs, workflows, and subagents.
- Do not add parallel registries, loaders, session stores, agent runtimes,
  capability frameworks, `DomainRegistry`, or `RoleRegistry`.
- Model a domain as an installable bundle/plugin plus agent role presets.
- Model a role as an existing agent preset with persona, prompts, skills,
  scoped tools, policies, and service requirements.
- Follow SOLID: keep contracts narrow, depend on service definitions rather
  than providers, keep policy separate from execution, and keep domain packages
  isolated from generic packages.

## Models, tools, and capabilities

- Keep canonical message, stream, and tool contracts independent of provider
  wire formats. Convert only inside provider adapters.
- Keep one canonical `ToolRuntime`. A model tool surface is a scoped view over
  canonical tools, never a second execution registry.
- Tool-surface aliases must preserve parameter schemas and arguments. Do not
  rewrite model arguments; use an explicit typed adapter tool when schemas
  differ.
- Register tools dynamically through Cordis effects and preserve typed input,
  typed output, timeout, cancellation, concurrency, presentation, and policy
  metadata.
- Tool implementations must consume generic capability services such as
  filesystem, subprocess, shell, sandbox, terminal, Web, jobs, and subagents.
- Never execute model output through `os.system`, unchecked shell
  interpolation, or an unvalidated process-launch path.
- Forward cancellation and wait for owned work to reach quiescence.

## Policy and security

- Prompts advise; policies and capability providers enforce.
- Preserve monotonic safety: an allow decision must never override a tool
  restriction, guard denial, sandbox denial, or provider boundary.
- Resolve and freeze effect/target metadata before approval and execution.
- Keep effect metadata host-only; never leak it into provider tool schemas.
- Preserve logged arguments = executed arguments.
- Treat undeclared MCP/third-party tools as opaque in strict profiles.
- Treat Cordis scope as composition isolation, not an untrusted-code sandbox.
- Do not claim local filesystem sandboxing enforces network scope.
- Keep unrestricted Cybersecurity shell, PTY, scanners, credentials, and MCP
  disabled until provider-level target/egress enforcement exists.
- Redact credentials and sensitive values before prompt, result, or audit
  persistence.
- Use Sentinel only as an architectural reference. Its audited repository has
  no source license grant; do not copy its source, prompts, or schemas.

## Compatibility and upstream maintenance

- Preserve existing profiles, presets, plugins, model adapters, tools,
  sessions, SDKs, MCP integrations, and CLI behavior by default.
- Use identity/permissive defaults for additive generic seams and keep domain
  behavior opt-in.
- Do not rename or move upstream files without a demonstrated requirement.
- Keep Cybersecurity code under `packages/domain/`; never import it from core.
- Keep generic policy extensions in the existing guard subsystem.
- Record every unavoidable core modification in an upstream-style architecture
  or agent note and add adjacent regression tests.
- Preserve full upstream Git history and merge upstream updates; do not replace
  upstream code with copied snapshots.

## Verification

- Test with real Cordis loader composition, not only hand-built contexts.
- Cover unit, integration, sandbox/process, profile-isolation, session replay,
  SDK, and provider-adapter behavior in proportion to each phase.
- Include deterministic `LLM → tool → policy → provider → result → LLM`
  coverage for new vertical slices.
- Verify Cybersecurity loading and unloading does not mutate `standard`
  general/coding behavior.
- Document deferred or unsafe capabilities as disabled; do not imply they are
  implemented or secure.
